{"acceptedResponses":["E"],"answer":"E","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"\\(4\\)","B":"\\(3\\)","C":"\\(2\\)","D":"\\(1\\)","E":"\\(0\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"1970_AMC_12_Q11","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor"],"contentHash":"8b35f53f50527050333aef733433e8a6dfca8e8da0b6a1a9b5e5c52c629162e2","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitute-factor-roots","label":"Substitute the factor roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-factor-roots":["cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_090"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-missing-quadratic-coefficient","label":"Use the missing x-squared term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-missing-quadratic-coefficient":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_146"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_029","cnm_032","cnm_042","cnm_008"],"id":"substitute-factor-zeros","label":"Substitute the zeros supplied by the factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-factor-zeros":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_090","cnm_029","cnm_032","cnm_042","cnm_008"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_042","cnm_029","cnm_008"],"id":"match-factor-coefficients","label":"Use the missing quadratic term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-factor-coefficients":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146","cnm_032","cnm_042","cnm_029","cnm_008"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_029","cnm_032","cnm_008"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":11,"question":"If two factors of \\(2x^3-hx+k\\) are \\(x+2\\) and \\(x-1,\\) the value of \\(|2h-3k|\\) is:","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"A polynomial becomes zero when one of its factors becomes zero. Thus the factors \\(x+2\\) and \\(x-1\\) tell us to substitute \\(x=-2\\) and \\(x=1\\) into \\(2x^3-hx+k\\). This gives\n\\[\n-16+2h+k=0,\n\\qquad\n2-h+k=0,\n\\]\nor equivalently,\n\\[\n2h+k=16,\n\\qquad\nh-k=2.\n\\]\nAdding the equations eliminates \\(k\\), giving \\(3h=18\\), so \\(h=6\\). Then \\(h-k=2\\) gives \\(k=4\\). Therefore\n\\[\n|2h-3k|=|12-12|=0,\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_090"],"explanation":"A polynomial becomes zero when one of its factors becomes zero. Thus the factors \\(x+2\\) and \\(x-1\\) tell us to substitute \\(x=-2\\) and \\(x=1\\) into \\(2x^3-hx+k\\). This gives\n\\[\n-16+2h+k=0,\n\\qquad\n2-h+k=0,\n\\]\nor equivalently,\n\\[\n2h+k=16,\n\\qquad\nh-k=2.\n\\]\nAdding the equations eliminates \\(k\\), giving \\(3h=18\\), so \\(h=6\\). Then \\(h-k=2\\) gives \\(k=4\\). Therefore\n\\[\n|2h-3k|=|12-12|=0,\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).","id":"substitute-factor-zeros","label":"Substitute the zeros supplied by the factors","legacyRouteId":"substitute-factor-zeros","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1970_AMC_12_Q11.json","sha256":"c1420fdaa502aa7aeed7a83af906da0bf6586b6c7cae3955615f15e9a76c5b3e"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/1970_AMC_12_Q11#substitute-factor-zeros","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146"],"explanation":"The two given factors are distinct, so their product divides the cubic. Its leading coefficient is \\(2\\), so the remaining factor has the form \\(2x+t\\), where \\(t\\) is a constant:\n\\[\n2x^3-hx+k=(x+2)(x-1)(2x+t).\n\\]\nThe missing \\(x^2\\) term will determine \\(t\\). Expanding gives\n\\[\n(x^2+x-2)(2x+t)\n=2x^3+(t+2)x^2+(t-4)x-2t.\n\\]\nSince the original polynomial has \\(x^2\\)-coefficient zero, \\(t+2=0\\), so \\(t=-2\\). Comparing the remaining coefficients gives\n\\[\n-h=t-4=-6,\n\\qquad\nk=-2t=4.\n\\]\nHence \\(h=6\\), \\(k=4\\), and\n\\[\n|2h-3k|=|12-12|=0,\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).","id":"match-factor-coefficients","label":"Use the missing quadratic term","legacyRouteId":"match-factor-coefficients","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1970_AMC_12_Q11.json","sha256":"c1420fdaa502aa7aeed7a83af906da0bf6586b6c7cae3955615f15e9a76c5b3e"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/1970_AMC_12_Q11#match-factor-coefficients","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_090"],"id":"substitute-factor-roots","label":"Substitute the factor roots","legacyRouteId":"substitute-factor-roots","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph and grading review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1970_AMC_12_Q11.json","sha256":"c1420fdaa502aa7aeed7a83af906da0bf6586b6c7cae3955615f15e9a76c5b3e"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","source":"Existing reviewed concept route","sourceRef":"amc/1970_AMC_12_Q11#substitute-factor-roots"}},{"directNodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_146"],"id":"use-missing-quadratic-coefficient","label":"Use the missing x-squared term","